11
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:41,400
Volume Profile Trading Tool.

12
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:45,832
The volume profile provides an objective visual structure

13
00:00:45,832 --> 00:00:49,064
to market activity that reflects the embodiment of

14
00:00:49,064 --> 00:00:52,700
the actions of all time frames and market participants.

15
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:59,900
Once you have learned to read and interpret the volume profile, it becomes possible to understand and interpret market behavior.

16
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:02,000
What this means is very simple.

17
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:04,823
If you are able to understand volume profile

18
00:01:04,823 --> 00:01:07,346
and understand the various patterns that the volume

19
00:01:07,346 --> 00:01:09,869
profile will show you, you will be able

20
00:01:09,869 --> 00:01:14,284
to understand what the 5 different time frame participants are doing at any given moment.

21
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:22,200
Being able to understand this will put you in an extremely strong position to interpret future direction of prices.

22
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:26,600
Note, volume profiling is made up of three elements.

23
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:30,000
The first is price. Price advertises opportunity.

24
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,300
It's made up of time. Time regulates each one of these opportunities.

25
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:44,400
And volume. Volume measures the degree of success of each auction.

26
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:51,700
Volume over each price shows willingness or unwillingness of participants to transact at various prices.

27
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:55,900
We'll talk more about this as we go through the volume profiling section.

28
00:01:58,400 --> 00:01:59,379
Volume Profile Metrics

29
00:01:59,379 --> 00:02:06,885
Here is an exhaustive list of the volume profile metrics you can expect to see not just in this module but across all

30
00:02:06,885 --> 00:02:09,496
the modules as we go over this course.

31
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:12,660
Do take note, whilst this list

32
00:02:12,660 --> 00:02:15,540
seems quite extensive and confusing, we will start

33
00:02:15,540 --> 00:02:19,500
to break them down individually over the next couple of slides.

34
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:23,400
So what are the key identifiable volume profile metrics?

35
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:28,200
The first is the open, the close, the high and the low.

36
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:31,388
As the name suggests, this is where the

37
00:02:31,388 --> 00:02:33,576
market opens on the day, where it closes

38
00:02:33,576 --> 00:02:35,764
on the day, the highest price traded on

39
00:02:35,764 --> 00:02:38,499
the day and the lowest price traded on the day.

40
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:45,900
The next key identifiable metric is the daily range, that is the high minus the low.

41
00:02:47,400 --> 00:02:53,800
Then we have the initial balance range. This is the first hour of trading within any market.

42
00:02:54,500 --> 00:03:01,300
This is usually depicted in traditional European markets as the K and the K. L TPO.

43
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:04,500
TPO stands for time price opportunity.

44
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:08,700
Again, we will talk about this further in further slides to come.

45
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:12,100
Then we have the cash range.

46
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:19,597
Now the cash range is typically the hour after the initial balance. So it's the second hour of trading in the day.

47
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:24,900
This traditionally gets depicted with an M and an N TPO print.

48
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:28,600
Note however that it doesn't have to be an M and an N.

49
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:30,300
It can be any letter.

50
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:35,200
We are just for ease of understanding we are going to use M and N.

51
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:40,600
We then have the volume value area plus traditional value areas.

52
00:03:40,900 --> 00:03:49,800
The volume value area is simply the area of the distribution where 70% of the day's volume is transacted at.

53
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:57,454
In terms of a traditional value area, this is just 70% of the time of the day where the market transacts at.

54
00:04:00,100 --> 00:04:07,696
Then, the value area range. This is simply the distance between the high and the low of the value area of the day.

55
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:10,400
We then have single prints.

56
00:04:11,100 --> 00:04:20,400
Single prints are merely a single TPO or a single time price opportunity that exists within a daily profile.

57
00:04:20,700 --> 00:04:26,997
Again, we will talk more about this down the line, so do not worry too much if you don't understand this at the moment.

58
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,600
We are merely just going through the various ideas. identifiable metrics within volume profiles.

59
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:36,400
We then have low volume nodes and high volume nodes.

60
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:38,557
Low volume nodes is where

61
00:04:38,557 --> 00:04:41,017
little or very little volume trades over

62
00:04:41,017 --> 00:04:43,125
a pair of a day, where

63
00:04:43,125 --> 00:04:45,233
high volume nodes is where a

64
00:04:45,233 --> 00:04:48,396
high amount of volume is transacted over a day.

65
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:52,900
We then have tails. These can be buying tails or selling tails.

66
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:57,300
Buying tails and selling tails need to be confirmed before it's a tail.

67
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:03,500
The time of the day for a buying or selling tail is quite important as well as the cause of the tail.

68
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:07,800
Again, we will understand why this is important as we move further along.

69
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:12,400
We then have the POC, also known as the point of control.

70
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:16,000
We have the VPOC, which is the volume point of control.

71
00:05:16,400 --> 00:05:19,316
The difference is the point of control

72
00:05:19,316 --> 00:05:22,232
takes into account only the time price

73
00:05:22,232 --> 00:05:26,398
opportunities, whereas the volume point of control factors in volume.

74
00:05:28,464 --> 00:05:32,264
We then have the profile shape. What is the shape of the distribution?

75
00:05:32,564 --> 00:05:37,664
Now this can be a number of different shapes and again we will talk about this extensively down the line.

76
00:05:37,864 --> 00:05:44,064
We're merely just identifying what are the key metrics found within a traditional volume profile.

77
00:05:44,564 --> 00:05:46,664
And last but not least, the volume.

78
00:05:46,964 --> 00:05:54,262
The volume is merely how much or how many contracts are transacted at each price over the course of the day.

79
00:05:54,864 --> 00:05:59,264
Let's now move on and break down and have a look at a basic volume profile.

80
00:06:01,064 --> 00:06:07,959
Now that we've dealt with each one of the individual metrics, let's see what they look like in a volume profile and where you can find them.

81
00:06:08,864 --> 00:06:14,264
As you can see in front of you, we have a traditional volume profile or a distribution.

82
00:06:14,964 --> 00:06:18,464
As you can see on the right of this distribution, we have prices.

83
00:06:19,064 --> 00:06:21,364
In the middle, we have all sorts of letters.

84
00:06:21,764 --> 00:06:28,364
Now, how a volume profile works is every single session is represented by a letter.

85
00:06:28,764 --> 00:06:30,964
Now, this session can vary in duration.

86
00:06:31,464 --> 00:06:37,364
Traditionally, most traders represent each letter represents a 30 minute time frame.

87
00:06:37,664 --> 00:06:44,064
However, we could use either weekly or monthly or longer term time frames. So it all depends.

88
00:06:44,364 --> 00:06:49,664
What we will be teaching you predominantly throughout this course is a day to day profile.

89
00:06:50,164 --> 00:06:53,864
And therefore, each letter represents a 30 minute time frame.

90
00:06:54,264 --> 00:07:00,864
Now, remember, we said a volume profile. We have price, we have time and we have volume.

91
00:07:01,264 --> 00:07:03,464
As you can see on the right we have prices.

92
00:07:04,064 --> 00:07:09,764
In the middle with the bars, with the horizontal bars, that is the volume traded at each price.

93
00:07:10,064 --> 00:07:12,364
And then we have the individual letters.

94
00:07:13,464 --> 00:07:17,064
Let's now look at the individual parts of the volume profile.

95
00:07:17,564 --> 00:07:25,061
So first up, we can see the open. The open price is represented with a big O. This is where the trading session begins.

96
00:07:25,564 --> 00:07:29,464
The times for the individual markets vary, so do be aware.

97
00:07:30,264 --> 00:07:34,364
We then have the close. The close is represented with a star.

98
00:07:34,864 --> 00:07:39,164
Now do note again, closes vary depending on which market you trade.

99
00:07:39,564 --> 00:07:46,661
This star will be continuously moving throughout the day as the market trades and will only stop once the market officially shuts.

100
00:07:47,364 --> 00:07:51,572
The next metric is the high of the day. As you can see,

101
00:07:51,572 --> 00:07:57,461
the high is at the TPO or the letter that trades at the highest price of the session.

102
00:07:57,964 --> 00:08:04,960
And then the low of the day. The low is the price, the lowest traded price on the day in that session, in that time frame.

103
00:08:06,464 --> 00:08:10,864
We then have the range. The daily range is merely the high minus the low.

104
00:08:12,564 --> 00:08:18,664
We then have the initial balance range. The initial balance is the first hour of any trading session.

105
00:08:18,964 --> 00:08:22,964
Now this depends again in Europe, this can be from 7 to 8 a.m.

106
00:08:23,164 --> 00:08:26,864
In the US, this can be from the overnight session from 11 to 8 a.m.

107
00:08:26,864 --> 00:08:27,664
to 12 p.m.

108
00:08:27,864 --> 00:08:30,164
So it all depends on which market you're trading.

109
00:08:30,664 --> 00:08:34,449
Do note, depending on which market you're trading, you should

110
00:08:34,449 --> 00:08:37,477
know the opening and closing hours, as this

111
00:08:37,477 --> 00:08:41,262
will obviously adjust your charts according to the time frames.

112
00:08:41,564 --> 00:08:44,456
So the initial balance is represented with this long blue

113
00:08:44,456 --> 00:08:49,663
bar, and that is the first hour of trade or where the first hour of trade is contained.

114
00:08:51,064 --> 00:08:57,964
We then have the cash range. The cash range traditionally is the first hour of trade once the cash begins.

115
00:08:58,364 --> 00:09:04,564
Now again, in the European session the cash trade tends to open an hour after the futures open.

116
00:09:05,164 --> 00:09:08,964
In US this is very different. So again, note the time differences.

117
00:09:09,264 --> 00:09:16,362
If you're unsure, you can find out more information on either the CME website, the Eurex website or the Life website.

118
00:09:17,764 --> 00:09:20,164
We then have the volume value area.

119
00:09:20,264 --> 00:09:26,564
Now the volume value area is the point where 70% of the day's volume is transacted.

120
00:09:26,764 --> 00:09:30,564
Now note on this chart I've colored it in a purple color.

121
00:09:30,864 --> 00:09:34,122
This is so it's easily identifiable where 70% of the

122
00:09:34,122 --> 00:09:40,638
day's range or the 70% of the day's volume is transacted or within which range is that volume transacted.

123
00:09:42,364 --> 00:09:44,964
Then we have the single prints.

124
00:09:44,964 --> 00:09:47,535
Now, single prints is where there

125
00:09:47,535 --> 00:09:53,963
is only a single TPO or a single letter that is represented on the chart.

126
00:09:54,164 --> 00:10:01,664
The reason we highlight these single prints is a lot of the... the time these single prints can tell us a lot about what's going on within the auction process.

127
00:10:01,864 --> 00:10:09,464
We will discuss this a little bit further on, but do note single prints can be highly important and that is why we mention them.

128
00:10:10,964 --> 00:10:16,564
We then have the high volume node and the low volume node.

129
00:10:16,864 --> 00:10:20,264
A high volume node is where a lot of volume is trans...

130
00:10:21,364 --> 00:10:24,805
So now that we've seen the volume profile as an

131
00:10:24,805 --> 00:10:27,557
entire distribution, I'm now going to show you

132
00:10:27,557 --> 00:10:33,062
how we can open up the profile and view each single auction within the day's distribution.

133
00:10:33,464 --> 00:10:37,864
So what we're going to do is we're just going to split the profile per auction.

134
00:10:38,064 --> 00:10:40,600
Again, later on I will show you how

135
00:10:40,600 --> 00:10:43,136
you can set up your profiles, how you

136
00:10:43,136 --> 00:10:45,672
can manage your profiles, adjust them according to

137
00:10:45,672 --> 00:10:47,574
how you want to view them

138
00:10:47,574 --> 00:10:49,476
and utilize them in such a

139
00:10:49,476 --> 00:10:52,963
manner that you can get highly effective information out of them.

140
00:10:54,464 --> 00:10:56,339
Note, each TPO time price

141
00:10:56,339 --> 00:10:59,714
observation represents a 30 minute auction with the open price

142
00:10:59,714 --> 00:11:04,589
illustrated with an O and the closed price where the next TPO opens.

143
00:11:04,964 --> 00:11:08,664
So note, what you're seeing here visually is nothing more

144
00:11:08,664 --> 00:11:11,624
than the previous distribution except we've now opened

145
00:11:11,624 --> 00:11:15,694
it up to view every single TPO or every single 30 minute

146
00:11:18,064 --> 00:11:20,664
TPO that occurred over the course of the session.

147
00:11:21,164 --> 00:11:29,059
As you can see, each one begins with an O and each one closes where the next price letter starts with an O.

148
00:11:31,264 --> 00:11:34,615
Now, the reason we have a look at the individual

149
00:11:34,615 --> 00:11:37,966
auctions and why they're important is that we are able

150
00:11:37,966 --> 00:11:43,663
to see strengths and weaknesses within each auction that takes place over the course of the day.

151
00:11:43,964 --> 00:11:46,906
So sometimes a lot of traders will just merely look

152
00:11:46,906 --> 00:11:48,965
at their profiles closed up for the

153
00:11:48,965 --> 00:11:51,024
entire session and they miss a lot

154
00:11:51,024 --> 00:11:54,260
of information that is happening within each one of the auctions.

155
00:11:54,464 --> 00:12:00,064
So it's important to find a balance between opening up the profiles and keeping them closed for shape.

156
00:12:01,464 --> 00:12:06,264
Note, viewing the auction process in this manner will illustrate four key points.

157
00:12:06,464 --> 00:12:11,764
The first is, when either buyer or seller is in control, it can last for a few TPOs.

158
00:12:12,164 --> 00:12:15,964
Now, as a rule of thumb, I have a little bit of an expression or a saying.

159
00:12:16,264 --> 00:12:18,247
When a market is moving in one

160
00:12:18,247 --> 00:12:19,663
direction, it's a lot like

161
00:12:19,663 --> 00:12:23,062
a big ship, a big tanker, oil tanker coming into a harbour.

162
00:12:23,464 --> 00:12:29,664
Note, it takes a long, long time for an oil tanker to turn around. Just like the markets.

163
00:12:29,764 --> 00:12:35,264
The markets very rarely turn around and very aggressively reverse in one direction.

164
00:12:35,464 --> 00:12:40,864
So by opening up the profiles, we're able to see the individual auctions taken in place.

165
00:12:41,164 --> 00:12:47,760
And when a buyer or a seller is in control, it takes quite a long time for it to actually eventually turn around.

166
00:12:48,364 --> 00:12:50,558
Knowing this is extremely powerful information because it

167
00:12:50,558 --> 00:12:52,752
means that even if we wish to go

168
00:12:52,752 --> 00:12:57,963
counter trend, sometimes we just have to be a little bit patient for an oil tanker to turn around.

169
00:12:56,964 --> 00:12:58,064
our trade to play out.

170
00:12:59,364 --> 00:13:01,564
The next reason that we want to illustrate

171
00:13:01,564 --> 00:13:09,264
it this way around is that we see most of the two-way trade occurring where price is being accepted and the least trade where price is being rejected.

172
00:13:09,464 --> 00:13:17,164
So by opening it up we can see where price is being very, where the market accepts price, where buyers and sellers are happily

173
00:13:17,165 --> 00:13:23,262
transacting with one another and equally we can see where buyers and sellers are not transacting with one another.

174
00:13:23,464 --> 00:13:28,982
In the example we can see here where we created these two tails with the A print at the bottom

175
00:13:28,982 --> 00:13:31,741
and the Y print at the top, we can see

176
00:13:31,741 --> 00:13:33,672
with the A print that buyers very

177
00:13:33,672 --> 00:13:35,603
quickly rejected low prices and at the

178
00:13:35,603 --> 00:13:38,361
top we can see sellers very quickly rejected higher prices.

179
00:13:38,564 --> 00:13:42,164
So by opening it up we can visually see this rejection of prices.

180
00:13:43,464 --> 00:13:49,464
The next reason we want to split is that we can identify hidden auction tails as mentioned before.

181
00:13:50,664 --> 00:13:53,764
And these auction tails will show a strength from buyers and sellers.

182
00:13:54,664 --> 00:14:00,964
And the fourth reason is we can see where price volatility expands and contracts.

183
00:14:01,164 --> 00:14:02,164
What do we mean by that?

184
00:14:02,364 --> 00:14:04,170
In other words, when we

185
00:14:04,170 --> 00:14:06,699
have small ranges within an auction, as

186
00:14:06,699 --> 00:14:09,589
you can see, we have small ranges initially

187
00:14:09,589 --> 00:14:13,563
in this auction for the first couple of half hour bars.

188
00:14:14,264 --> 00:14:19,464
And then the market suddenly, when we have the V print, the market suddenly opens up.

189
00:14:19,464 --> 00:14:22,064
So we can see a sudden shift in volatility.

190
00:14:22,264 --> 00:14:26,864
So opening up the auction process in this way will show us when there's suddenly...

191
00:14:26,964 --> 00:14:31,164
a shift in the auction that is occurring between buyers and sellers.

192
00:14:31,264 --> 00:14:37,864
And this sort of information is extremely vital to us as traders when making trading decisions.

